href="/assets/navy-metal.css"><link rel="stylesheet" href="/assets/calculi.css"><link rel="stylesheet" href="/assets/refactor.css"><link rel="stylesheet" href="/assets/refactor-matchings.css"><script defer src="/assets/build-a-commutator.js"></script><script defer src="/assets/analytics.js" data-tensorgami-analytics="v1"></script></head><body><a class="skip" href="#main">Skip to content</a><div class="wrap"><header class="masthead"><a class="brand" href="/" aria-label="Refactor home"><img class="brand-emblem" src="/assets/brand/refactor-mark.svg?v=8be7135d0d95" width="48" height="48" alt="Refactor monogram" fetchpriority="high">Refactor</a><nav class="nav" aria-label="Main navigation"><a href="/papers">Research</a><a href="/expositions" aria-current="true">Mathematics</a><a href="/essays">Essays</a><a href="/about">About</a></nav></header><main id="main"><header class="refactor-article-heading"><p class="eyebrow"><a href="/expositions">Mathematics</a> / Groups · Matchings</p><h1>Build a commutator from two matchings</h1><p class="lead">Turn a group equation into a balance of red and blue edges. Recover a commutator and find the fewest swaps needed in its first, involutive factor.</p><p class="byline">Henry Shin · November 21, 2026 · 11 min read</p><p class="reader-background"><strong>Begin with:</strong> Permutation cycles and composition. Commutators and matchings are introduced in the article.</p><div class="text-links"><a href="/assets/refactor/build-a-commutator.md" download>Download reading text</a><a href="/expositions">All mathematics</a></div></header><div class="refactor-reading-layout"><aside class="refactor-reading-sidebar"><nav aria-label="Article contents"><a href="#experiment-title">Balance two cycles in S6</a><a href="#return">Read the relabeling back into the group</a><a href="#move">Change the factors while holding the product fixed</a><a href="#theorem">Balance every even permutation</a><a href="#what-transfers">What has to come back</a><a href="#matching-sequel">Continue with perfect matchings</a><a href="#sources">Sources</a></nav><a href="/refactor">The Refactor approach</a></aside><article class="refactor-prose"><p class="article-lead">Take the permutation <span class="cycle">(1 2)(3 4 5 6)</span>. Applying it is easy. Taking it apart is harder. Can it be written as a single commutator, the product of two moves followed by their inverses in the same order?</p>
<p>We write <span class="cycle">[<var>α</var>, <var>τ</var>] = <var>α</var><var>τ</var><var>α</var><sup>−1</sup><var>τ</var><sup>−1</sup></span> and apply the rightmost permutation first. Try asking for a little more. Require <span class="cycle"><var>α</var><sup>2</sup> = 1</span>, allowing the identity as the empty matching. Then the equation for the target <var>π</var> becomes</p>
<div class="math-display" data-tex="\pi=\alpha\beta,\qquad\beta=\tau\alpha\tau^{-1}.">
<math display="block" aria-label="pi equals alpha beta, where beta equals tau alpha tau inverse">
    <mi>π</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>α</mi><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mspace width="1.1em"></mspace>
    <mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>τ</mi><mi>α</mi><msup><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>.</mo>
  </math>
</div>
<p>An involution swaps disjoint pairs and fixes the remaining points. Draw its swaps as edges, one per pair. This is a matching. Conjugating it by <var>τ</var> sends every edge <span class="cycle">{<var>u</var>, <var>v</var>}</span> to <span class="cycle">{<var>τ</var>(<var>u</var>), <var>τ</var>(<var>v</var>)}</span>. So here is the problem on the other side of the bridge. Find two matchings whose product is <var>π</var> and whose edge counts agree. Equal counts let us pair up the edges and then pair up the isolated vertices. Those pairings supply the vertex bijection <var>τ</var>.</p>

      <section class="refactor-matching-experiment" id="try-it" aria-labelledby="experiment-title">
<h2 id="experiment-title">Balance two cycles in <i>S</i><sub>6</sub></h2>
<p>Both paths start with one extra red edge. Give one cycle an extra blue edge instead. Pick a starting vertex to see the blue step followed by the red step. At a missing edge, stay at the same vertex.</p>
